.TH BYTE_CASE_START 3 "2023-04-01" "1.0" "lib/byte/byte_case_start"
.SH NAME
byte_case_start \- Compare the start of two strings case-insensitively
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/byte.h>

.IB size_t
.IB byte_case_start(const void *x1, size_t len, const void *x2);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbyte_case_start\fR function compares the start of two strings \fIx1\fR and \fIx2\fR of length \fIl\fR in a case-insensitive manner. It returns 1 if the first \fIl\fR bytes of \fIx1\fR match the start of the string \fIx2\fR, and 0 otherwise.

This function is useful for comparing the start of strings without considering their case.
.SH ARGUMENTS
\fIx1\fR
The first string to compare.
\fIl\fR
The length of the strings.
\fIx2\fR
The second string to compare.
.SH RETURN VALUE
1 if the first \fIl\fR bytes of \fIx1\fR match the start of the string \fIx2\fR, 0 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbyte_case_start\fR function:
.nf
#include <lib/byte.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    const char *str1 = "Hello";
    const char *str2 = "hello world";

    if (byte_case_start(str2, 5, str1)) {
        printf("Start of strings match case-insensitively\n");
    } else {
        printf("Start of strings differ case-insensitively\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
.ne
.fi
